                id: 'lc2_96',
                title: 'Lesson 96 - The dead return',
                passage: 'A Festival for the Dead is held once a year in Japan. This festival is a cheerful occasion, for on this day, the dead are said to return to their homes and they are welcomed by the living. As they are expected to be hungry after their long journey, food is laid out for them. Specially-made lanterns are hung outside each house to help the dead to find their way. All night long, people dance and sing. In the early morning, the food that had been laid out for the dead is thrown into a river or into the sea as it is considered unlucky for anyone living to eat it. In towns that are near the sea, the tiny lanterns which had been hung in the streets the night before, are placed into the water when the festival is over. Thousands of lanterns slowly drift out to sea guiding the dead on their return journey to the other world. This is a moving spectacle, for crowds of people stand on the shore watching the lanterns drifting away until they can be seen no more.',
                passageCn: '日本每年举行一次"亡灵节"。这个节日是个欢乐的日子，因为在这一天，据说死去的人要回到他们的家里来，活着的人则对他们表示欢迎。因为预料到他们在经过长途跋涉之后会感到饥饿，所以为他们摆出了食物。家家户户都悬挂着特制的灯笼，帮助死者认路。人们整夜地跳舞、唱歌。第二天一大早，为死者摆好的食物被扔进河里或海里，因为人们认为活着的人吃了这些东西是不吉利的。在靠海的城镇，头天夜里挂在大街小巷的小灯笼在节日结束后被放到了水里。成千上万只灯笼慢慢漂向大海，指引着亡灵返回另一个世界。这是一个感人的场面，人们成群地伫立在海岸上，注视着灯笼远去，直到再也看不见为止。',
